// =====================================================================
// PROPOSED Firestore security rules for stotramulu (project stotramulu-eddf4)
//
// HOW TO PASTE THIS INTO THE FIREBASE CONSOLE
//   1. Open https://console.firebase.google.com -> project "stotramulu-eddf4"
//      -> Build -> Firestore Database -> the "Rules" tab.
//   2. Copy the rules that are live now into a text file first, so you can
//      paste them back if anything misbehaves.
//   3. Select everything in the editor and paste this WHOLE file in its
//      place (these comment lines are fine to keep). The paste must
//      include the line  rules_version = '2';  just above `service`.
//   4. Before pressing "Publish", open "Rules Playground" and try the
//      checks listed at the end of this file.
//   5. Press "Publish". Every client picks the change up within a minute.
//   6. Then run the live-site tests listed at the end of this file.
//
// DEPLOY ORDER (conversations and attachments, Phase 27)
//   Paste and publish these rules FIRST, and only then deploy the new site.
//   These rules still accept everything the site that is live today writes
//   (the 14 old feedback fields, the old reply / status writes), so the old
//   site keeps working in the meantime. The old rules would reject the new
//   site's writes (lastAt, claimKey, files, messages, attachments), and a
//   rejected message is dropped silently, so the other order loses messages.
//
// WHAT EACH COLLECTION IS FOR (read with the code that talks to Firestore)
//   users/{uid}     cloud.js: each signed-in reader syncs { track, email,
//                   updatedAt } to their OWN document. Nobody else, not even
//                   the admin, needs to read it.
//   stotras/{id}    admin.js lists the WHOLE collection for every visitor and
//                   skips `published == false` in the browser, so reads must
//                   stay public (a published-only rule would fail that query).
//                   Drafts are therefore readable by anyone who asks.
//   config/{doc}    weekday.js reads config/weekday for every visitor.
//   updates/{id}    updates.js shows these under the bell; the dashboard
//                   (admin-dashboard.js) is the only writer and always writes
//                   the full shape { date, tag, title, body, published,
//                   updatedAt }.
//   feedback/{id}   One conversation. cloud.js lets anyone (signed in or not)
//                   start one, and the first message IS this document
//                   (`message`, plus a `files` list when it has attachments).
//                   A reader who is not signed in also sends a random
//                   `claimKey`, which lets them claim the conversation when
//                   they later sign in on the same phone (messages.js).
//                   status / reply / repliedAt / lastAdminAt / claimProof can
//                   NEVER be sent on create.
//                   A signed-in reader can read back their own conversations
//                   (messages.js: where('uid', '==', uid), no orderBy).
//                   Three kinds of update are allowed, each with its own
//                   short list of fields: the admin's triage and reply
//                   (admin-dashboard.js), the owner's follow-up
//                   (messages.js) and the claim on sign-in (messages.js).
//     .../messages/{mid}   The follow-up messages of that conversation, in
//                   the order of `createdAt`. The signed-in owner writes
//                   `from: 'user'` ones (messages.js), the admin writes
//                   `from: 'admin'` ones (admin-dashboard.js). They are never
//                   edited; only the admin deletes them, together with the
//                   conversation.
//     .../files/{fileId}   One attachment each (attachments.js upload()):
//                   the image or PDF itself is stored in the `data` field as
//                   Bytes, because Firebase Storage needs the paid plan. A file
//                   must first be declared in the `files` list of the message
//                   it belongs to. It is written once, never edited; only the
//                   admin deletes it. The same people who may read the
//                   conversation may read its messages and files.
//
// KEEP IN STEP WITH THE CODE
//   feedback.js (the send form and its offline queue) retries a queued
//   message with set() on the same id. If that doc already exists, the
//   retry is an *update*. None of the three update paths below accepts it
//   (a retry always carries a new createdAt), so it is denied, and
//   feedback.js drops the queue entry on permission-denied (that is
//   intended). cloud.js __cloudSendReport() (a first message with
//   attachments) reads the same permission-denied as "the conversation is
//   already there" only when an earlier try of that message may have
//   landed, and then goes on to the attachments. On a first try it means
//   the rules refused the message, and the reader is told it did not send.
//   The flip side: a queued message that FAILS VALIDATION is also dropped
//   silently. So, and always publish the rules FIRST (see DEPLOY ORDER):
//   - a new field in cloud.js's feedback `doc`   -> add it to validNewFeedback();
//   - a new field in the admin's status / reply write (admin-dashboard.js)
//                                                 -> add it to validTriage();
//   - a new field in the reader's follow-up or claim write (messages.js)
//                                -> add it to validOwnerFollowUp() / validClaim();
//   - a new field in a follow-up or reply message -> add it to validMessage();
//   - a change to the entries of a `files` list. validMeta() mirrors
//     StotramFiles.meta() in attachments.js, which builds them, and
//     cloud.js cleanFilesMeta(), which passes them on: exactly
//     { id, name, type, size }, a name of up to 120 characters, one of the
//     four types, and a whole number of bytes   -> change validMeta();
//   - a change to StotramFiles.LIMITS in attachments.js (3 files, 800000
//     bytes, the four types), to StotramFiles.fileId() (mid + '-' + n) or to
//     the format of StotramFiles.newMessageId()   -> change validMeta(),
//     validFile() and the id check on messages create the same way.
//
// COST (the free Spark plan allows 50,000 document reads a day)
//   Every get() inside a rule is billed as one document read, on top of the
//   documents the request itself reads. These rules make at most two get()
//   calls per request:
//   - a reader opening their thread or one attachment: 1 (the conversation);
//   - the owner adding a follow-up message: 1 (the conversation);
//   - an attachment of the first message: 1 (the conversation);
//   - an attachment of a later message: 1 for the admin (the message), 2 for
//     the owner (the message and the conversation);
//   - everything else the admin does (reading, deleting, status changes,
//     the reply batch itself): 0.
//   A file document is at most about 800 KB, under Firestore's 1 MiB limit.
//
// ABUSE AND STORAGE (read this once before publishing)
//   The risk. Starting a conversation needs no sign-in, and neither do the
//   up to 3 attachments of that first message (800,000 bytes each, sent
//   within 15 minutes). The site's Firebase web key is public (it is in
//   every page), so a script can do this again and again. Rules cannot
//   count or slow down requests, and they cannot tell whether the bytes
//   really are a picture or a PDF: they only check the declared type and
//   the size. One such conversation stores about 2.4 MB, so a little over
//   400 of them fill the free plan's 1 GiB of stored data. That 1 GiB is
//   shared by everything in the database (stotras, config, updates, users
//   and feedback). Every text field is capped, and so is each entry of a
//   `files` list (validMeta), so text alone cannot fill it quickly.
//   What it looks like when the 1 GiB is full. Firestore refuses new writes
//   (the browser console shows quota errors such as "resource-exhausted")
//   until the stored data is back under the limit: new messages and
//   attachments fail, the dashboard cannot reply or save, and signed-in
//   readers' practice sync (users/) stops saving. Reading the site keeps
//   working. Text-only messages wait in the phone's queue and are sent
//   again later; a message with attachments shows its error to the reader.
//   Firestore -> Usage in the console shows the stored size (the figure is
//   not live and can lag behind).
//   How to recover. In the dashboard's Feedback tab, delete the junk
//   conversations one by one: Delete removes a conversation together with
//   its messages and files, and can simply be pressed again if it stops
//   half-way. Deletes have their own free allowance (20,000 a day); one
//   conversation with 3 attachments is about 4 deletes. Writes work again
//   once the stored data is under 1 GiB.
//   Optional hardening, later (a site change, not a rules change):
//   Firebase App Check with the reCAPTCHA v3 provider. Turn it on in
//   MONITOR mode first (App Check -> APIs -> Cloud Firestore stays
//   "Unenforced") and watch the verified / unverified figures for a week
//   or two. Enforcing it covers EVERY Firestore request, including the
//   public stotras and config reads, so older phones where reCAPTCHA fails
//   or is blocked would lose the whole site, not just the feedback form.
//   reCAPTCHA's free monthly allowance of checks also applies. Enforce it
//   only if nearly all real traffic shows as verified. Do not shrink the
//   not-signed-in size limit instead: the form promises PDFs up to 750 KB.
//
// The admin UID matches window.ADMIN_UID in public/assets/site-config.js.
// Anything not matched below is denied (Firestore's default).
// =====================================================================

// Keep the next line: without it Firebase assumes rules version 1 and
// rejects the `let` lines below. Copy from here down (or the whole file).
rules_version = '2';
service cloud.firestore {
  match /databases/{database}/documents {

    // ---------- helpers ----------
    function signedIn() {
      return request.auth != null;
    }
    function isAdmin() {
      return signedIn() && request.auth.uid == '0d3PPSYaFncy1tY2oUGtBMGMFwu2';
    }
    function isStr(v, max) {
      return v is string && v.size() <= max;
    }
    // Field is absent, or a string no longer than max.
    function optStr(d, key, max) {
      return !(key in d) || isStr(d[key], max);
    }
    // Field is absent, null, or a string no longer than max.
    function optStrOrNull(d, key, max) {
      return !(key in d) || d[key] == null || isStr(d[key], max);
    }

    // ---------- users: each reader's own synced practice data ----------
    match /users/{uid} {
      allow read, write: if signedIn() && request.auth.uid == uid;
    }

    // ---------- stotras: public read, admin write ----------
    match /stotras/{id} {
      allow read: if true;
      allow write: if isAdmin();
    }

    // ---------- config (weekday map): public read, admin write ----------
    match /config/{doc} {
      allow read: if true;
      allow write: if isAdmin();
    }

    // ---------- updates ("What's new"): public read, admin write ----------
    function validUpdate(d) {
      return d.keys().hasOnly(['date', 'tag', 'title', 'body', 'published', 'updatedAt'])
        && d.keys().hasAll(['date', 'tag', 'title', 'published'])
        && d.date is string && d.date.matches('^[0-9]{4}-[0-9]{2}-[0-9]{2}$')
        && d.tag in ['new', 'improved', 'fixed']
        && isStr(d.title, 200) && d.title.size() > 0
        && optStr(d, 'body', 2000)
        && d.published is bool
        && (!('updatedAt' in d) || d.updatedAt == request.time);
    }
    match /updates/{id} {
      allow read: if true;
      allow create, update: if isAdmin() && validUpdate(request.resource.data);
      allow delete: if isAdmin();
    }

    // ---------- the `files` list of a message ----------
    // The first message of a conversation and every follow-up or reply can
    // list up to 3 attachments as [{id, name, type, size}], built by
    // StotramFiles.meta() in attachments.js. Each entry is checked on its
    // own: every other field has a size cap, and without this check a
    // 3-entry list of long strings could fill a whole document.
    // f['size'] is read with brackets because size() is also a map method.
    function validMeta(f) {
      return f is map
        && f.keys().hasOnly(['id', 'name', 'type', 'size'])
        && f.keys().hasAll(['id', 'name', 'type', 'size'])
        && isStr(f.id, 80)
        && isStr(f.name, 120)
        && f.type in ['image/webp', 'image/jpeg', 'image/png', 'application/pdf']
        && f['size'] is int
        && f['size'] >= 0
        && f['size'] <= 800000;
    }
    // A list of at most 3 entries, each one valid. Rules have no loops, so
    // the three places are checked one by one. An empty list is fine.
    function validMetaList(l) {
      return l is list
        && l.size() <= 3
        && (l.size() < 1 || validMeta(l[0]))
        && (l.size() < 2 || validMeta(l[1]))
        && (l.size() < 3 || validMeta(l[2]));
    }

    // ---------- feedback: one conversation per document ----------
    // Exactly the fields cloud.js writes, nothing more. `status`, `reply`,
    // `repliedAt`, `lastAdminAt` and `claimProof` are not in the list, so a
    // create carrying any of them is denied; `handled` must be the default
    // false. The last four names in the list are new in Phase 27 and are all
    // optional, so the 14-field document that the site live today writes
    // still passes.
    function validNewFeedback(d) {
      return d.keys().hasOnly([
            'type', 'name', 'contact', 'message', 'screen', 'stotram', 'stotramTitle',
            'lang', 'device', 'sentAt', 'uid', 'email', 'handled', 'createdAt',
            'lastAt', 'lastFrom', 'claimKey', 'files'])
        && d.keys().hasAll(['type', 'message', 'createdAt'])
        && d.type in ['correction', 'problem', 'suggestion', 'other']
        && d.message is string && d.message.size() > 0 && d.message.size() <= 20000
        && optStr(d, 'name', 500)
        && optStr(d, 'contact', 500)
        && optStr(d, 'screen', 300)
        && optStr(d, 'stotram', 200)
        && optStr(d, 'stotramTitle', 500)
        && optStr(d, 'lang', 50)
        && optStr(d, 'device', 1000)
        && optStrOrNull(d, 'sentAt', 64)
        // Anonymous (null) is always fine; a uid must be the sender's own.
        && (!('uid' in d) || d.uid == null || (signedIn() && d.uid == request.auth.uid))
        // A signed-in sender's email must be their account's email.
        && (!('email' in d) || d.email == null
            || (signedIn() && d.email == request.auth.token.get('email', null)))
        && (!('handled' in d) || d.handled == false)
        && d.createdAt == request.time
        // New (Phase 27), all optional. lastAt / lastFrom: when the
        // conversation last moved and who moved it (serverTimestamp(), 'user').
        && (!('lastAt' in d) || d.lastAt == request.time)
        && (!('lastFrom' in d) || d.lastFrom == 'user')
        // claimKey: only on a message sent without signing in. It is the
        // secret the same phone shows later to claim the conversation
        // (validClaim), so it must be long and random: 32-64 hex characters.
        && (!('claimKey' in d)
            || (d.get('uid', null) == null
                && d.claimKey is string
                && d.claimKey.matches('^[0-9a-fA-F]{32,64}$')))
        // files: the attachments of this first message, at most 3, each
        // entry checked by validMeta(). Each one is uploaded afterwards as
        // its own files/first-<n> document (see validFile).
        && (!('files' in d) || validMetaList(d.files));
    }

    // Update path 1 - admin triage and reply (admin-dashboard.js):
    //   status change -> { status, handled }
    //   reply         -> batch: a new messages/{mid} document (see below) and
    //                    { status: 'answered', handled: true,
    //                      lastAt: serverTimestamp(), lastFrom: 'admin',
    //                      lastAdminAt: serverTimestamp() }
    //   legacy reply  -> { reply, repliedAt: serverTimestamp(), status:
    //                    'answered', handled: true } (the dashboard that is
    //                    live today; the new one never writes `reply`)
    function validTriage() {
      let d = request.resource.data;
      let changed = d.diff(resource.data).affectedKeys();
      return changed.hasOnly(['status', 'handled', 'reply', 'repliedAt',
                              'lastAt', 'lastFrom', 'lastAdminAt'])
        && (!('status' in d) || d.status in ['new', 'in_progress', 'answered', 'closed'])
        && (!('handled' in d) || d.handled is bool)
        && (!('reply' in d) || isStr(d.reply, 5000))
        && (!changed.hasAny(['repliedAt']) || d.repliedAt == request.time)
        && (!('lastAt' in d) || d.lastAt is timestamp)
        && (!('lastFrom' in d) || d.lastFrom in ['user', 'admin'])
        && (!changed.hasAny(['lastAdminAt']) || d.lastAdminAt == request.time);
    }

    // Update path 2 - the owner's follow-up (messages.js). A signed-in
    // reader writing back in their own conversation sends one batch: a new
    // messages/{mid} document (see below) and exactly this patch, which puts
    // the conversation back in the admin's "new" pile:
    //   { lastAt: serverTimestamp(), lastFrom: 'user', status: 'new',
    //     handled: false }
    // (The rule cannot see the message in the same batch, so an owner could
    // send this patch alone. That only re-opens their own conversation.)
    function validOwnerFollowUp() {
      let d = request.resource.data;
      return signedIn()
        && resource.data.get('uid', null) == request.auth.uid
        && d.diff(resource.data).affectedKeys().hasOnly(['lastAt', 'lastFrom', 'status', 'handled'])
        && d.lastAt == request.time
        && d.lastFrom == 'user'
        && d.status == 'new'
        && d.handled == false;
    }

    // Update path 3 - the claim (messages.js, when the reader signs in). A
    // conversation started without signing in has uid == null and a stored
    // claimKey. The phone that sent it kept the key, and on sign-in sends
    //   { uid: <their uid>, email: <their email or null>, claimProof: <the key> }
    // Only someone holding the key can do this: until the claim, only the
    // admin can read the conversation or its files, so nobody else sees it.
    // After the claim uid is no longer null, so it can never be claimed again.
    // A claim is accepted up to 30 days after the message was sent.
    function validClaim() {
      let d = request.resource.data;
      let stored = resource.data;
      return signedIn()
        && stored.get('uid', null) == null
        && stored.get('claimKey', null) is string
        && stored.claimKey.size() >= 32
        && d.diff(stored).affectedKeys().hasOnly(['uid', 'email', 'claimProof'])
        && d.uid == request.auth.uid
        && d.get('claimProof', null) == stored.claimKey
        && (d.get('email', null) == null
            || d.email == request.auth.token.get('email', null))
        && stored.createdAt > request.time - duration.value(30, 'd');
    }

    // ---------- the conversation behind a message or a file ----------
    // Each call to conversation() is one billed read (see COST at the top).
    function conversation(fbid) {
      return get(/databases/$(database)/documents/feedback/$(fbid)).data;
    }
    // Signed in, and the owner of conversation fbid. A conversation started
    // without signing in has no owner (uid == null) until it is claimed.
    function ownsConversation(fbid) {
      return signedIn() && conversation(fbid).get('uid', null) == request.auth.uid;
    }

    // ---------- messages: follow-ups, append-only ----------
    // { from: 'user' | 'admin', uid, text (0-5000 characters),
    //   files: [{id, name, type, size}] (0-3, optional, each entry checked
    //   by validMeta(); messages.js sends files: [] when nothing is
    //   attached, the dashboard leaves the key out),
    //   createdAt: serverTimestamp() }
    // The text may be empty only when at least one file is declared.
    function validMessage(d) {
      return d.keys().hasOnly(['from', 'uid', 'text', 'files', 'createdAt'])
        && d.keys().hasAll(['from', 'uid', 'text', 'createdAt'])
        && d.from in ['user', 'admin']
        && d.text is string && d.text.size() <= 5000
        && (!('files' in d) || validMetaList(d.files))
        && (d.text.size() > 0 || d.get('files', []).size() > 0)
        && d.createdAt == request.time;
    }
    // Who may post: the admin as 'admin', or the conversation's signed-in
    // owner as 'user'; either way `uid` is the sender's own uid. The admin
    // check comes first, so the admin's reply costs no extra read.
    function canPostMessage(fbid, d) {
      return signedIn()
        && d.uid == request.auth.uid
        && ((d.from == 'admin' && isAdmin())
            || (d.from == 'user' && ownsConversation(fbid)));
    }

    // ---------- files: one attachment per document, create-only ----------
    // { mid: 'first' | <message id>, n: 0 | 1 | 2 (a whole number), name (up to 120),
    //   type: 'image/webp' | 'image/jpeg' | 'image/png' | 'application/pdf',
    //   size: <number of bytes in data>, data: Bytes (up to 800000),
    //   from: 'user' | 'admin', createdAt: serverTimestamp(),
    //   key: <the conversation's claimKey> (only when not signed in) }
    // The document id must be mid + '-' + n (first-0, m-1790000000000-a1b2c3-2),
    // so each file slot can be written exactly once. `n is int` matters:
    // Firestore counts 0.0 as equal to 0, and string(0.0) is '0.0', so
    // without it a script could add extra slots such as first-0.0.
    // `size` and `data` are read with d['...'] on purpose: `size` is also
    // the name of the map method size(), and the brackets keep that apart.
    function validFile(d, fileId) {
      return d.keys().hasOnly(['mid', 'n', 'name', 'type', 'size', 'data',
                               'from', 'createdAt', 'key'])
        && d.keys().hasAll(['mid', 'n', 'name', 'type', 'size', 'data',
                            'from', 'createdAt'])
        && d.mid is string
        && d.mid.matches('^(first|m-[0-9]{10,16}-[a-z0-9]{4,10})$')
        && d.n is int
        && d.n in [0, 1, 2]
        && fileId == d.mid + '-' + string(d.n)
        && isStr(d.name, 120)
        && d.type in ['image/webp', 'image/jpeg', 'image/png', 'application/pdf']
        && d['data'] is bytes
        && d['data'].size() <= 800000
        && d['size'] == d['data'].size()
        && d.from in ['user', 'admin']
        && optStr(d, 'key', 64)
        && d.createdAt == request.time;
    }
    // An attachment of the FIRST message (mid == 'first'). It must be
    // declared in the conversation's own `files` list (n < its length), and
    // written by one of:
    //   - the admin, as 'admin';
    //   - the conversation's signed-in owner, as 'user' (cloud.js, signed in;
    //     or a retry after the claim);
    //   - the phone that just sent it without signing in, as 'user': the
    //     conversation still has no owner, the file carries the
    //     conversation's claimKey as `key`, and the conversation was created
    //     less than 15 minutes ago (cloud.js uploads right after sending).
    // p = the conversation's stored data.
    function firstFileOk(d, p) {
      return d.n < p.get('files', []).size()
        && ((d.from == 'admin' && isAdmin())
            || (d.from == 'user' && signedIn()
                && p.get('uid', null) == request.auth.uid)
            || (d.from == 'user'
                && p.get('uid', null) == null
                && p.get('claimKey', null) is string
                && d.get('key', null) == p.claimKey
                && p.createdAt > request.time - duration.value(15, 'm')));
    }
    // An attachment of a follow-up or reply message (mid == a message id).
    // It must be declared in that message's `files` list, have the same
    // `from` as the message (so nobody can slip a file into the other
    // side's message), and be written by the admin as 'admin' or by the
    // conversation's signed-in owner as 'user'.
    // m = the message's stored data.
    function messageFileOk(fbid, d, m) {
      return d.n < m.get('files', []).size()
        && d.from == m.from
        && ((d.from == 'admin' && isAdmin())
            || (d.from == 'user' && ownsConversation(fbid)));
    }
    // Shape first (no reads), then one get() for the document that declares
    // the file: the conversation for 'first', else the message. Only a
    // reader's file on a later message needs a second get() (the
    // conversation, for the owner check).
    function canCreateFile(fbid, fileId) {
      let d = request.resource.data;
      return validFile(d, fileId)
        && ((d.mid == 'first'
             && firstFileOk(d, conversation(fbid)))
            || (d.mid != 'first'
                && messageFileOk(fbid, d,
                     get(/databases/$(database)/documents/feedback/$(fbid)/messages/$(d.mid)).data)));
    }

    match /feedback/{fbid} {
      allow create: if fbid.size() <= 100 && validNewFeedback(request.resource.data);
      allow read: if isAdmin()
        || (signedIn() && resource.data.uid == request.auth.uid);
      allow update: if (isAdmin() && validTriage())
        || validOwnerFollowUp()
        || validClaim();
      allow delete: if isAdmin();

      // Follow-up messages. Read (one message or the whole list): the
      // admin, or the conversation's owner. Created only under an id of the
      // shape StotramFiles.newMessageId() makes, m-<milliseconds>-<4 to 10
      // of a-z 0-9>, the same pattern validFile() allows for `mid`: a
      // message under any other id could never get its files, so it is
      // refused here, and the whole batch (message + parent patch) fails
      // before anything is stored. Never edited. Deleted only by the admin
      // (admin-dashboard.js deletes every message and file of a
      // conversation before the conversation itself).
      match /messages/{mid} {
        allow read: if isAdmin() || ownsConversation(fbid);
        allow create: if mid.matches('^m-[0-9]{10,16}-[a-z0-9]{4,10}$')
          && validMessage(request.resource.data)
          && canPostMessage(fbid, request.resource.data);
        allow update: if false;
        allow delete: if isAdmin();
      }

      // Attachments. Read: the admin, or the conversation's owner. Written
      // once (canCreateFile), never edited, deleted only by the admin.
      match /files/{fileId} {
        allow read: if isAdmin() || ownsConversation(fbid);
        allow create: if canCreateFile(fbid, fileId);
        allow update: if false;
        allow delete: if isAdmin();
      }
    }
  }
}

// ---------------------------------------------------------------------
// Rules Playground checks worth running (Firestore -> Rules -> Rules playground).
// Type the path into "Location", set "Authenticated" as shown and press Run
// after each one. "Admin" means Authenticated on with Firebase UID
// 0d3PPSYaFncy1tY2oUGtBMGMFwu2. test123 and m-1 are made-up ids that do not
// exist, which is the point: these results do not depend on your data.
//   get    /stotras/test                    Authenticated off     -> Allowed
//   get    /updates/test                    Authenticated off     -> Allowed
//   get    /users/abc                       on, Firebase UID abc  -> Allowed
//   get    /users/abc                       on, Firebase UID xyz  -> Denied
//   get    /feedback/test123                Authenticated off     -> Denied
//   get    /feedback/test123                Admin                 -> Allowed
//   get    /feedback/test123/messages/m-1   Authenticated off     -> Denied
//   get    /feedback/test123/messages/m-1   Admin                 -> Allowed
//   get    /feedback/test123/messages/m-1   on, Firebase UID xyz  -> Denied
//   get    /feedback/test123/files/first-0  Authenticated off     -> Denied
//   get    /feedback/test123/files/first-0  Admin                 -> Allowed
//   get    /feedback/test123/files/first-0  on, Firebase UID xyz  -> Denied
//   delete /feedback/test123/files/first-0  on, Firebase UID xyz  -> Denied
//   delete /feedback/test123/files/first-0  Admin                 -> Allowed
//   delete /feedback/test123/messages/m-1   Admin                 -> Allowed
//   delete /feedback/test123                Admin                 -> Allowed
// These need "Build document", but only to have something to send (add one
// string field, text = "x"); they must be Denied whatever the document says:
//   update /feedback/test123/messages/m-1   Admin                 -> Denied
//          (nobody edits a message, not even the admin)
//   update /feedback/test123/files/first-0  Admin                 -> Denied
//          (nobody edits an attachment)
//   update /feedback/test123                Authenticated off     -> Denied
//          (a reader who is not signed in can never change a conversation)
// (Playground simulations are not saved, so the "delete" checks delete nothing.)
//
// What the Playground CANNOT test: every create here (and the reply,
// follow-up and claim updates) needs createdAt / lastAt to equal the server
// clock (request.time), which the site sets with serverTimestamp() but a
// typed-in timestamp never matches. Test those on the live site instead.
//
// Live-site tests, part 1 - right after publishing, while the OLD site is
// still live (this proves the old site's writes still pass):
//   1. Without signing in, send a message -> it appears in the dashboard
//      (a rejected message is dropped silently, so do not skip this).
//   2. Signed in, send a message, then open "My messages" -> it is there.
//   3. In the dashboard, change that message's status and send a reply ->
//      both work, and the reply shows up in "My messages".
//   4. Add an entry in the dashboard's Updates tab -> it shows under the bell.
// Live-site tests, part 2 - after deploying the NEW site:
//   5. Without signing in, send a message with a screenshot and a small PDF
//      -> the dashboard's conversation shows the text and both attachments
//      (the create with claimKey + files, then files/first-0 and first-1
//      written by the not-signed-in path).
//   6. On the same phone, sign in with Google and open "My messages" -> that
//      message is listed, and its attachments open (the claim worked; in the
//      console's Data tab the document now has uid and claimProof).
//   7. Reply from the dashboard with a picture -> the dashboard shows it as
//      answered; on the phone the unread dot appears, and the thread shows
//      the reply with its picture (the admin's reply batch and upload; the
//      owner reading messages and files).
//   8. On the phone, write back with a picture -> the dashboard shows the
//      conversation under "Waiting" with the new message and picture (the
//      owner's follow-up batch and upload).
//   9. Signed in from the start, send a new message with a screenshot -> the
//      dashboard shows it with the screenshot (the owner path for first-0).
//  10. Change a conversation's status in the dashboard -> it moves to the
//      matching filter.
//  11. Delete a test conversation from the dashboard -> in the console's Data
//      tab, the document and its messages and files are all gone.
// The 15-minute window for uploads without signing in and the 30-day
// window for claims cannot be tested quickly on the live site; check them
// by reading firstFileOk() and validClaim() above. The same goes for the
// checks that only a hand-made request can trip (each `files` entry in
// validMeta(), a whole-number `n` in validFile(), the message id pattern
// on messages create): the site never sends such values, so on the live
// site they only have to let the site's own writes through, which tests
// 5 to 9 above show.
// ---------------------------------------------------------------------
